The content was read by OCR, so it may contain errors. Write a title that says what the document is, who issued it and what it concerns, so it can be found again in a long list.

The original title is often a file name or a mail subject. Use it only for facts it adds, such as a reference number or a period, and take neither its wording nor its language.

<language_rule>
Write the title in the language of the document's main body text, because the archive keeps every document in its own language and a translated title no longer matches the words inside the document when it is searched. Decide the language from the body paragraphs alone and ignore everything around them: mail headers and forwarding lines (From, To, Subject, Von, An, Betreff, Weitergeleitete Nachricht), the original title, the reader's locale, and the German category names this archive uses. Keep the document's own terms, product names and date format.
</language_rule>

